  "summary": "The Trump administration is intensifying pressure on healthcare providers through multiple enforcement mechanisms, with the affordability czar warning over 500 hospitals to disclose pricing information under threat of fines while separately pushing Medicaid cuts that executives are scrambling to mitigate. Meanwhile, the biotech sector continues consolidating, with GSK acquiring cancer drug developer Nuvalent for $10.6 billion and Incyte buying Vega Therapeutics for $1.25 billion to add bleeding disorder and oncology assets. Artificial intelligence is increasingly embedding itself across healthcare IT, with EHR vendors and imaging companies adopting agentic AI tools, though successful clinical integration still hinges on clinician trust and proper alert design to enhance rather than burden workflows. Separately, persistent drug shortages remain a systemic problem despite a 23% decline last year, and wearable devices are beginning to integrate with clinical systems as platforms like Oura and Whoop move to bring real-world health data into patient care.",
